// **The `else` the declaration-attribute chain went without.** Every other attribute position
// refuses a name it does not know -- a field attribute against what the generators declare
// (`derive_validate.zm`'s `attributes()`), a module attribute against its four, a variant
// attribute against `#[from]` -- and this one simply fell off the end of an `if`/`else if` chain.
//
// So `#[foo_bar] struct User` compiled silently, which is `#[deprecated]`'s own history: an
// attribute the compiler accepts while nothing reads it is indistinguishable from one that works.
//
// **Which of the nine applies where is a separate question**, answered after the declaration is
// known -- `#[test]` on a struct and `#[thread_bound]` on a function are each refused by name
// further down. This is only "is it an attribute at all", which is the one thing knowable while
// the attribute is being read.
#[foo_bar]
struct User {
    name: string
}

fn main() {
    print(1)
}
